Note



Letters and number are called literal characters - they match exactly. Punctuation characters .,+, *, [, ], are metacharacters - have special meaning.


E.g., . will match any character - a. matches string containing “a” followed by any character:

str_view(c("a", "ab", "ae", "bd", "ea", "eab"), pattern = "a.")

[2] │ &lt;ab&gt;
[3] │ &lt;ae&gt;
[6] │ e&lt;ab&gt;

str_view(fruit, pattern = "a...e")

 [1] │ &lt;apple&gt;
 [7] │ bl&lt;ackbe&gt;rry
[48] │ mand&lt;arine&gt;
[51] │ nect&lt;arine&gt;
[62] │ pine&lt;apple&gt;
[64] │ pomegr&lt;anate&gt;
[70] │ r&lt;aspbe&gt;rry
[73] │ sal&lt;al be&gt;rry


Quantifiers control the times a pattern can match: + ? makes pattern optional - either matches 0 or 1 time + + lets pattern repeat - matches at least once + * lets pattern be optional or repeat

# ? matches "a" optionally followed by "b"
str_view(c("a", "ab", "abb"), pattern = "ab?")

[1] │ &lt;a&gt;
[2] │ &lt;ab&gt;
[3] │ &lt;ab&gt;b

# + matches "a" followed by at least one "b"
str_view(c("a", "ab", "abb"), pattern = "ab+")

[2] │ &lt;ab&gt;
[3] │ &lt;abb&gt;

# * matches "a" followed by any number of b's
str_view(c("a", "ab", "abb"), pattern = "ab*")

[1] │ &lt;a&gt;
[2] │ &lt;ab&gt;
[3] │ &lt;abb&gt;


Character classes defined by [] - matches set of characters, e.g., [abcd] matches “a”, “b”, “c”, or “d” - 1 matches anything except “a”, “b”, “c”, or “d”:

# Example: Finding words where x is surrounded by vowels

str_view(words, pattern = "[aeiou]x[aeiou]")

[284] │ &lt;exa&gt;ct
[285] │ &lt;exa&gt;mple
[288] │ &lt;exe&gt;rcise
[289] │ &lt;exi&gt;st


Can also use alternations | - choosing one or more alternative patterns:

str_view(fruit, pattern = "apple|melon|nut")

